1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,429
Every server lives on a network,

2
00:00:02,429 --> 00:00:04,478
and the network is its front door.

3
00:00:04,478 --> 00:00:06,105
Today you learn to read it,

4
00:00:06,105 --> 00:00:06,949
log in safely,

5
00:00:06,949 --> 00:00:09,119
and close the doors you do not need.

6
00:00:09,669 --> 00:00:12,191
You will see your addresses and routes,

7
00:00:12,191 --> 00:00:13,743
check what is listening,

8
00:00:13,743 --> 00:00:16,588
log in with S S H keys instead of passwords,

9
00:00:16,588 --> 00:00:18,335
lock down the S S H server,

10
00:00:18,335 --> 00:00:19,951
and switch on a firewall.

11
00:00:20,501 --> 00:00:25,579
It all runs on a practice network built on this computer, so nothing real is exposed.

12
00:00:27,629 --> 00:00:33,560
First, your addresses. Type: ip -br addr. Dash br gives a short table.

13
00:00:34,110 --> 00:00:35,885
Lo is the loopback,

14
00:00:35,885 --> 00:00:38,782
this machine talking to itself.

15
00:00:38,782 --> 00:00:41,118
Eth0 is the network card.

16
00:00:41,118 --> 00:00:43,454
Its address is 10.0.2.15,

17
00:00:43,454 --> 00:00:45,416
with a slash 24 mask.

18
00:00:46,366 --> 00:00:49,417
Next, where does traffic go? Type: ip route.

19
00:00:49,967 --> 00:00:53,869
The default route sends everything else to the gateway,

20
00:00:53,869 --> 00:00:54,507
10.0.2.2.

21
00:00:54,507 --> 00:00:58,692
The second line says the local network is reached directly.

22
00:00:59,642 --> 00:01:07,173
Can we reach the gateway? Type: ping -c 2 10.0.2.2. Dash c two sends two packets.

23
00:01:07,723 --> 00:01:11,456
Two sent, two received, no loss. The time is in milliseconds.

24
00:01:12,406 --> 00:01:15,339
What is listening for connections?

25
00:01:15,339 --> 00:01:17,064
Type: sudo ss -tlnp.

26
00:01:17,064 --> 00:01:18,012
T is T C P,

27
00:01:18,012 --> 00:01:19,306
L is listening,

28
00:01:19,306 --> 00:01:20,427
N is numbers,

29
00:01:20,427 --> 00:01:22,497
and P shows the process.

30
00:01:23,047 --> 00:01:25,881
The program sshd is listening on port 22,

31
00:01:25,881 --> 00:01:27,539
on the loopback address.

32
00:01:27,539 --> 00:01:29,129
Know what is listening,

33
00:01:29,129 --> 00:01:31,410
and you know your attack surface.

34
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:36,975
S S H keys replace passwords.

35
00:01:36,975 --> 00:01:38,429
Make a pair.

36
00:01:38,429 --> 00:01:46,671
Type: ssh-keygen -q -t ed25519 -N empty quotes -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.

37
00:01:46,671 --> 00:01:51,883
The empty passphrase is for this demo only.

38
00:01:51,883 --> 00:01:55,519
Use a passphrase on real keys.

39
00:01:56,069 --> 00:02:03,066
Dash q keeps it quiet, so nothing prints. List the folder. Type: ls ~/.ssh.

40
00:02:03,616 --> 00:02:04,261
Two files.

41
00:02:04,261 --> 00:02:08,004
The private key stays on your machine and is never shared.

42
00:02:08,004 --> 00:02:10,263
The dot pub file is the public key,

43
00:02:10,263 --> 00:02:11,360
safe to hand out.

44
00:02:12,310 --> 00:02:20,253
The server decides who may log in with a file called authorized keys.

45
00:02:20,253 --> 00:02:23,246
Add your public key to it.

46
00:02:23,246 --> 00:02:28,886
Type: cat ~/.ssh/*.pub >> ~/.ssh/authorized_keys.

47
00:02:29,436 --> 00:02:31,652
Lock the file down.

48
00:02:31,652 --> 00:02:36,199
Type: chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys.

49
00:02:36,199 --> 00:02:41,447
S S H refuses keys that other users can read.

50
00:02:41,997 --> 00:02:42,813
Now log in.

51
00:02:42,813 --> 00:02:44,965
Type: ssh localhost hostname.

52
00:02:44,965 --> 00:02:48,305
That runs the hostname command on the server,

53
00:02:48,305 --> 00:02:50,679
which here is this same machine.

54
00:02:51,229 --> 00:02:52,220
The first time,

55
00:02:52,220 --> 00:02:56,647
S S H shows the server's fingerprint and asks whether you trust it.

56
00:02:56,647 --> 00:02:57,771
On a real server,

57
00:02:57,771 --> 00:02:59,819
compare it with a trusted copy.

58
00:02:59,819 --> 00:03:00,744
Then type yes.

59
00:03:04,294 --> 00:03:09,030
No password was asked. The key proved who you are, and the server printed its name.

60
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:17,241
The server's settings live in the S S H D config.

61
00:03:17,241 --> 00:03:20,007
Show the lab settings.

62
00:03:20,007 --> 00:03:25,288
Type: grep -h ^P /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.

63
00:03:25,838 --> 00:03:27,940
Password authentication is no,

64
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:31,234
so guessing passwords gets an attacker nowhere.

65
00:03:31,234 --> 00:03:32,916
Permit root login is no,

66
00:03:32,916 --> 00:03:35,368
so nobody logs in as root directly.

67
00:03:35,368 --> 00:03:37,401
Pubkey authentication is yes.

68
00:03:38,351 --> 00:03:46,884
What is the server really using? Type: sudo sshd -T | grep -E, and the pattern in quotes.

69
00:03:47,434 --> 00:03:53,023
Dash capital T prints the effective settings, after every file is merged. Both say no.

70
00:03:53,973 --> 00:03:55,550
Now test the lock.

71
00:03:55,550 --> 00:03:58,177
Try to log in without the key.

72
00:03:58,177 --> 00:04:02,293
Type: ssh -o PubkeyAuthentication=no localhost.

73
00:04:02,843 --> 00:04:03,958
Permission denied,

74
00:04:03,958 --> 00:04:04,639
public key.

75
00:04:04,639 --> 00:04:07,179
Passwords are off and no key was offered,

76
00:04:07,179 --> 00:04:08,603
so the door stays shut.

77
00:04:09,553 --> 00:04:17,553
Before you reload a real server, test the config. Type: sudo sshd -t && echo config ok.

78
00:04:18,103 --> 00:04:18,792
No errors,

79
00:04:18,792 --> 00:04:20,237
so it says config ok.

80
00:04:20,237 --> 00:04:22,233
A typo here can lock you out,

81
00:04:22,233 --> 00:04:23,266
so always test,

82
00:04:23,266 --> 00:04:25,399
and keep a second session open.

83
00:04:27,449 --> 00:04:32,317
A firewall decides which connections may reach the machine.

84
00:04:32,317 --> 00:04:33,142
On Ubuntu,

85
00:04:33,142 --> 00:04:34,957
U F W keeps it simple.

86
00:04:34,957 --> 00:04:36,772
Type: sudo ufw status.

87
00:04:37,322 --> 00:04:39,348
Inactive. Nothing is filtered yet.

88
00:04:40,198 --> 00:04:48,028
Allow S S H first, or you will lock yourself out. Type: sudo ufw allow 22/tcp.

89
00:04:48,578 --> 00:04:55,191
Rule added. Add web traffic too. Type: sudo ufw allow 80/tcp.

90
00:04:55,741 --> 00:04:57,041
Now switch it on.

91
00:04:57,041 --> 00:04:58,723
Type: sudo ufw enable.

92
00:04:58,723 --> 00:05:01,782
On a machine with an S S H session open,

93
00:05:01,782 --> 00:05:03,770
it may ask you to confirm,

94
00:05:03,770 --> 00:05:06,599
because a wrong rule can cut you off.

95
00:05:06,599 --> 00:05:08,128
You allowed port 22,

96
00:05:08,128 --> 00:05:09,352
so you are safe.

97
00:05:10,902 --> 00:05:11,373
Active,

98
00:05:11,373 --> 00:05:12,921
and enabled at startup.

99
00:05:12,921 --> 00:05:17,430
By default it denies incoming connections and allows outgoing ones.

100
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:23,308
List the rules with numbers. Type: sudo ufw status numbered.

101
00:05:23,858 --> 00:05:29,148
Two rules, ports 22 and 80, allowed in. Everything else coming in is denied.

102
00:05:30,098 --> 00:05:37,053
Remove a rule you no longer need. Type: sudo ufw delete allow 80/tcp.

103
00:05:37,603 --> 00:05:40,654
Rule deleted. Open only what the machine needs.

104
00:05:42,704 --> 00:05:44,688
A few habits stop most attacks.

105
00:05:45,238 --> 00:05:48,651
Use keys, not passwords, and turn password logins off.

106
00:05:49,201 --> 00:05:53,916
No direct root login. Use sudo, so every action is tied to a person.

107
00:05:54,466 --> 00:05:57,517
Open only the ports you need, and know what is listening.

108
00:05:58,067 --> 00:06:02,035
Keep the system updated, with the apt routine from lesson thirty-two.

109
00:06:02,585 --> 00:06:07,235
Give every user and service only the access it needs. That is least privilege.

110
00:06:09,285 --> 00:06:10,608
Four myths to clear up.

111
00:06:11,158 --> 00:06:14,021
Myth one: a firewall makes a server secure.

112
00:06:14,021 --> 00:06:14,221
No.

113
00:06:14,221 --> 00:06:15,286
It is one layer.

114
00:06:15,286 --> 00:06:18,283
Weak passwords and old software still get in.

115
00:06:18,833 --> 00:06:21,686
Myth two: long S S H passwords are enough.

116
00:06:21,686 --> 00:06:21,890
No.

117
00:06:21,890 --> 00:06:23,453
Keys cannot be guessed,

118
00:06:23,453 --> 00:06:25,830
and passwords can be tried all day.

119
00:06:26,380 --> 00:06:32,588
Myth three: changing the S S H port is security. No. It hides noise, but it is not a lock.

120
00:06:33,138 --> 00:06:34,965
Myth four: if it is listening,

121
00:06:34,965 --> 00:06:36,061
it must be needed.

122
00:06:36,061 --> 00:06:36,243
No.

123
00:06:36,243 --> 00:06:36,791
Check it,

124
00:06:36,791 --> 00:06:38,557
and stop what you do not use.

125
00:06:39,107 --> 00:06:40,686
Read it, lock it, filter it.

126
00:06:42,736 --> 00:06:45,061
Here is how you check your own understanding.

127
00:06:45,611 --> 00:06:49,195
Read your addresses and routes, and say what the default route does.

128
00:06:49,745 --> 00:06:52,241
List what is listening, and name the process.

129
00:06:52,791 --> 00:06:56,205
Log in with a key, and say why the private key stays put.

130
00:06:56,755 --> 00:07:00,957
Turn off password and root logins, and test the config before reloading.

131
00:07:01,507 --> 00:07:05,155
Allow S S H, then enable the firewall, and list the rules.

132
00:07:05,705 --> 00:07:07,561
Name three habits that cut risk.

133
00:07:08,111 --> 00:07:09,733
To pass, you need all six.

134
00:07:11,783 --> 00:07:12,447
Your turn,

135
00:07:12,447 --> 00:07:14,307
in your own virtual machine.

136
00:07:14,307 --> 00:07:17,098
Never practise on a server you do not own,

137
00:07:17,098 --> 00:07:21,682
and keep a second session open when you change S S H or the firewall.

138
00:07:22,232 --> 00:07:28,397
One: run ip -br addr, ip route and ss -tlnp, and write down what is listening.

139
00:07:28,947 --> 00:07:31,656
Two: make a key pair, with a passphrase this time.

140
00:07:32,206 --> 00:07:36,046
Three: add the public key to authorized keys, and log in with it.

141
00:07:36,596 --> 00:07:42,932
Four: in a second session, turn off passwords and root login, and test with sshd dash t.

142
00:07:43,482 --> 00:07:47,386
Five: allow S S H, enable the firewall, and list the rules.

143
00:07:47,936 --> 00:07:52,779
Six: confirm you can still log in from the second session before you close the first.

144
00:07:53,329 --> 00:07:56,636
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

145
00:08:12,186 --> 00:08:13,061
Quick check.

146
00:08:13,611 --> 00:08:15,360
Which command shows your addresses?

147
00:08:15,910 --> 00:08:18,129
Which file lists the keys allowed to log in?

148
00:08:18,679 --> 00:08:22,348
Which command tests the S S H config before you reload it?

149
00:08:22,898 --> 00:08:25,501
What must you allow before you enable the firewall?

150
00:08:26,051 --> 00:08:32,621
Objective covered: MC04-O09.

151
00:08:33,171 --> 00:08:35,241
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

152
00:08:45,791 --> 00:08:49,097
Next lesson: diagnose and recover a broken system.

153
00:08:49,647 --> 00:08:52,975
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
